Let $f_t : \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{Y}$ denote a (possibly black-box) predictive model at time $t \in \mathbb{N}$, deployed on inputs drawn from a time-varying distribution $P_t$ over $\mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{Y}$, and let $u_t : \mathcal{Y} \times \mathcal{A} \to \mathbb{R}$ be a time-varying utility function used by downstream decision-makers (with action set $\mathcal{A}$). A causal explanation method is a mapping $E$ that, given a query input $x \in \mathcal{X}$ and the triple $(f_t, P_t, u_t)$, returns an explanation $E(x; f_t, P_t, u_t)$ that purports to identify, in a causal sense, the features or mechanisms responsible for the prediction $f_t(x)$ and/or its utility consequences.

Most existing causal explanation methods are designed under the static assumption that $f_t = f$, $P_t = P$, and $u_t = u$ do not vary with $t$. Formulate and develop a theory of causal explanation methods $E$ that:

1. Remain valid under distribution shift, i.e. when $P_t \neq P_{t'}$ for $t \neq t'$ (covariate, label, or mechanism shift specified via a structural causal model);
2. Remain valid under model updates, i.e. when $f_t \neq f_{t'}$ (e.g. retraining, continual learning, or online updates);
3. Remain valid under changes in the utility function $u_t$ used to evaluate decisions based on the prediction.

Specifically: (a) Propose a precise definition of what it means for an explanation $E(x; f_t, P_t, u_t)$ to "remain valid over time" across such dynamics. (b) Construct an explanation method $E$ satisfying this definition under stated assumptions on the allowed dynamics (e.g. bounded mechanism shifts, smooth model updates), and prove guarantees (consistency, stability, or calibration) for it. (c) Identify impossibility results characterizing regimes of dynamics under which no such valid causal explanation method exists.

