Let $n\geq 3$ and consider a convex $n$-gon with vertices labeled $1,2,\dots,n$ in cyclic order. The (1-skeleton of the) associahedron $\mathcal{A}_n$ is the graph whose vertices are the triangulations of the $n$-gon and whose edges join two triangulations that differ by a single diagonal flip (removing a diagonal shared by two triangles and replacing it by the other diagonal of the resulting quadrilateral).

Fix an integer $k\geq 2$ and a coloring $c:\{1,\dots,n\}\to\{1,\dots,k\}$ assigning one of $k$ colors to each vertex of the $n$-gon. Call a triangulation $T$ of the $n$-gon $c$-colorful if every triangle of $T$ has vertices of at least two distinct colors (equivalently, no triangle of $T$ is monochromatic). Let $\mathcal{F}_n(c)$ denote the induced subgraph of $\mathcal{A}_n$ on the set of $c$-colorful triangulations.

When $k=2$ and $c$ is the alternating coloring on an even $n$, the graph $\mathcal{F}_n(c)$ is the previously studied "flip graph on colorful triangulations." The problem is:

Determine the structural properties of the induced subgraphs $\mathcal{F}_n(c)$ as $c$ ranges over arbitrary color sequences with $k=2$, and more generally as $k\geq 3$. In particular, investigate:

(a) connectivity and diameter of $\mathcal{F}_n(c)$;
(b) existence of Hamilton paths and Hamilton cycles in $\mathcal{F}_n(c)$ as a function of the coloring $c$ (e.g., as a function of the number and pattern of color changes around the $n$-gon);
(c) regularity, vertex-transitivity, and other symmetry properties;
(d) how these properties degrade or change when $k>2$ colors are used.

Provide a structural characterization or classification of these subgraphs in terms of the underlying coloring $c$.

