A star operation on an integral domain $D$ with quotient field $K$ is a map $\ast: \mathcal{F}(D) \to \mathcal{F}(D)$ on the set $\mathcal{F}(D)$ of nonzero fractional ideals of $D$ satisfying, for all $a \in K^\times$ and $I, J \in \mathcal{F}(D)$:
(i) $(aD)^\ast = aD$ and $(aI)^\ast = aI^\ast$;
(ii) $I \subseteq I^\ast$, and $I \subseteq J$ implies $I^\ast \subseteq J^\ast$;
(iii) $(I^\ast)^\ast = I^\ast$.

A theory of "projective star operations" has been developed in the setting of projective $n$-space $\mathbb{P}^n_K$, where the function field $K(x_1, \dots, x_n)$ is a purely transcendental extension of the base field $K$. In that setting one studies star-like closure operations on suitable classes of homogeneous (or graded) fractional ideals/divisorial sheaves compatible with the projective grading.

Problem: Extend the theory of projective star operations to arbitrary projective varieties. Equivalently, given a finitely generated field extension $F/K$ that is not assumed to be purely transcendental (so that $F$ is the function field of some projective variety over $K$), construct and develop a theory of projective star operations on appropriate classes of homogeneous fractional ideals or divisorial objects associated to $F/K$, generalizing all of the standard properties (such as the analogues of the $v$-, $t$-, and $w$-operations, the notion of $\ast$-invertibility, and the relevant class groups) known in the purely transcendental case.

